hi I'm Evan Carmichael welcome to an  edition of ask Evan today I mention a  question from one of our YouTube viewers  G West Oh want one who wrote in asking  about mastermind groups and the question  is I have a question that I would love  for you to comment on I was recently  reading a book that talked about the  mastermind circle how can I get or find  people that I can bounce ideas off of  and give advice to so thanks for the  question  awesome question I'm a firm believer in  mastermind groups and I've been running  a couple of them here in Toronto for the  past seven years I want to say one of  the things that I've done as part of the  website is we interview famous and  successful entrepreneurs and one of the  entrepreneur's were interviewed from  Western Canada has built this company  from 0 to 8 million a couple of years  and he credited his success to starting  mastermind groups and so that got me  interested in the concept as well as  reading Think and Grow Rich we actually  created a worksheet on it so there's  information how to launch and create  your own mastermind group but in terms  of kind of high-level overview of what  to do first I would look at you know are  there any groups in your area already  that exist and if there are it may be  worth investigating those instead of  creating your own because there's a lot  of headache work hassle and men in  setting up a group so there's something  that exists then that might be worth  investigating  I really prefer meetings in person I  know there are a lot of mass time groups  that meet online or by phone and they  can work but my personal preference is  to have ones that where you're actually  meeting face-to-face and I think the  reason is you get to know people a lot  better when you're sitting across from  the table from them and also you have  people's attention it's too easy when  you're on the phone or online to get  distracted check email to now go you  know check Facebook or some other  website and you hear clicking always  happening on the computer so I prefer to  have it in person but again explore your  options see what's out there if there's  nothing available in terms of creating  your own group I try to think about the  types of people that you want to have as  members and when I first created my  first group I did it by approaching a  few of my friends who are entrepreneurs  who I met with every now and then to  talk about business I thought if we had  a formal structure where we met  you know once a month to talk about our  businesses that would be helpful to  everybody and so a number of them agreed  and we started the first group around  that and then we expanded by bringing  new people in so I started with people  who are already kind of knew who I  wanted to tap and connect with on an  ongoing basis so think about the people  who you've met with in the past to talk  about business and may have gotten a few  ideas from them and you know they you've  given a few ideas to them as well that  could be a great way to start from there  I create even just a simple website and  most of the people who find our groups  now find it through the web whatever  area are in like say you're in Portland  if you created a website that had a page  that said Portland mastermind group  anybody searching for that kind of stuff  is gonna find you and chances are this  if you've already done your research and  you haven't joined another group because  there's nothing there  anybody who's searching for that is  gonna find you and so for us if people  search you know Toronto mastermind group  or Toronto peer mentorship group or  Toronto entrepreneur group the page  shows up and people self-identify and  write in to say I'm interested in  joining this group we have some  information on there and people end up  coming in you know potentially as  members of the group there's a whole  process that we go through to make sure  we get the right people in the group you  don't want the wrong dynamics or the  wrong types of people in the group  because I can bring the whole thing down  you have two people who don't like each  other so you have to really make sure  you do a lot of qualifying going through  that whole process I guess is another  another video we answered in the  worksheet as well but in terms of  finding people to come into the group  I again start with my friends people who  ie who I knew who I found some advice  helpful to and who I was able to help as  well and then expand it by creating a  simple website that explained what we  were about explain the types of people  were looking for and you want to be  really clear on the types of members you  want you know do you want startup  entrepreneurs you want people who are  beyond the setup stage you can have a  minimum revenue criteria or employee  criteria are there's types of businesses  you want to have certain industries that  you want to have or don't want to have  the more you can clearly lay that out  the more people can self-identify and  won't waste your time and won't waste  their time as well so for us for example  we looked for members who are beyond the  startup stage have $300,000 plus in  revenues and so that helps eliminate a  lot of people who may not be at that  point yet so I don't want to waste their  time and coming in to a meeting and you  know use up my time as well when it's  obviously not going to be a fit for the  group so the more you can be clear about  that criteria the easier it is for  people to self-identify before they even  write to you but having a simple website  that can be found in the search engines  people will start coming to you and you  can go through your process before  bringing them into the group again a  super helpful tool one that I definitely  recommend for is to go through because  it's it's hard and it's lonely and I  find with the mastermind group that the  two benefits are one you get the bounce  ideas off each other so you're thinking  of a problem that you're facing and if  you can surround yourself with the team  of people who have different skills than  you and different expertise and  different backgrounds they may be able  to help you and stretch you and help you  grow beyond what you thought you were  able to do which is great and the second  reason I find them the group's helpful  is just a motivation know there's  something about hanging around other  entrepreneurs that gets you excited and  gets your juices flowing and you hear  what they're up to and you want to do  more and do better and and get out of  whatever rut that you may be in at the  time a lot of mantras go through peaks  and valleys you know you get excited  you're working like crazy you're on top  of the world and then life sucks  nothing's happening you don't get  motivated to get up and out of bed it's  such a strange existence I guess but  that's that's the roller coaster that we  ride and I find that being around other  entrepreneurs gets the motivation going  gives you ideas give you that spark and  we always leave the meeting motivated  energized want to go out and achieve new  things with our businesses  so it can be definitely lonely being an  entrepreneur you may have friends and  family who understand what you're doing  but if they're not entrepreneurs they  can't really give you advice like they  can maybe give you an ear they can  listen to your problems but they can't  really help you and if you even if you  have staff or partner can often be hard  to talk to them about the problems that  you're facing in your business and so  having an external group people to to  turn to is awesome my last bit of advice  is on the selection side really make  sure you do your work in qualifying  people and you know both from us size of  business and type of business but also a  personality point of view we're never in  a rush to bring members into the group  well we'll start with with four and  build it to eight as a maximum but we're  happy to stick to four you know my  groups now are eight and six with two  potential people coming in but it's  never a rush the group with eight that  sold out we were at seven for a year and  a half just because they did love the  each other yes we had an open spot but  there was never a rush to fill that spot  it had to be the right person and so  it's much more valuable to get the right  people in the group and have fewer  people and just have somebody in the  chair because they're an extra space and  you want to try to get it up to eight or  ten or however many people you want in  your group so hopefully that helps again  love the concept and and I think it  could be helpful for you and for any  entrepreneur who's watching so thank you  guys for watching this video if you  liked it please give it a thumbs up  below I'd also love to hear what you  have to think if you have a comment  about this video or question about your  own business that I can answer for you  feel free to leave in the comment  section below I always read those and I  try to respond as quickly as I can and  I'd love to hopefully one day answer a  question from you about your business so  thank you and I'll see you on the next  episode  you
 

